 ## Overview
-This method renders volumetric data stored in a Sparse Voxel Octree (SVO) by decoupling the hierarchical traversal from the raycasting loop, using rasterization to cull empty space and shadow mapping for occlusion [S1, unofficial]. It solves the performance bottleneck of branch-intensive octree traversal on GPUs by rasterizing proxy geometry of a view-dependent cut of octree nodes to identify occupied bricks, then casting rays only through the resulting tight segments [S2, abstract only, unofficial]. Unlike sibling methods that traverse the tree per-ray, this approach uses a rasterization pass to generate per-pixel lists of bricks, enabling two levels of empty space skipping (brick and macrocell) and achieving 2 to 4 times faster rendering than state-of-the-art algorithms on large out-of-core datasets [S2, abstract only, unofficial].
+This method implements high-performance GPU volume rendering by decoupling the branch-intensive octree traversal from the raycasting loop, using a rasterization pass to identify active voxels and a shadow mapping pass for lighting [S1, unofficial][S2, abstract only, unofficial]. It solves the performance bottleneck of traditional raycasting on sparse data by moving empty-space culling into a graphics pipeline stage, achieving 2 to 4 times faster rendering speeds than state-of-the-art algorithms on large out-of-core datasets [S2, abstract only, unofficial]. Unlike sibling methods that rely on pure ray-tracing traversal or fixed-step DDA, this approach uses view-dependent proxy geometry rasterization to generate a per-pixel list of penetrated bricks, ensuring front-to-back processing and tighter ray segments [S2, abstract only, unofficial].
 
